diff --git a/website/docs/developer-guide/memory-provider-plugin.md b/website/docs/developer-guide/memory-provider-plugin.md
index 9ea6b0bd88..7d97374f5f 100644
--- a/website/docs/developer-guide/memory-provider-plugin.md
+++ b/website/docs/developer-guide/memory-provider-plugin.md
@@ -8,6 +8,81 @@ description: "How to build a memory provider plugin for Hermes Agent"
 
 Memory provider plugins give Hermes Agent persistent, cross-session knowledge beyond the built-in MEMORY.md and USER.md. This guide covers how to build one.
 
+## Explicit records from orchestration plugins
+
+Conversation capture and built-in memory mirroring do not constitute an arbitrary
+record-writing API. Providers can separately opt in to explicit records using
+these additive `MemoryProvider` methods (older providers keep inert defaults):
+
+* `record_capabilities()` returns boolean `write`, `lookup`, and `idempotency`
+  flags. Keep this description free of network calls and secret reads.
+* `record_destination()` returns a non-secret SHA-256 binding of the initialized
+  writer's actual endpoint/tenant/write-bank set and identity-sensitive policy.
+  Pin that destination to the instance. Reconfiguration must change the binding.
+* `write_record(record, *, effect_id, expected_destination, timeout)` atomically
+  checks the expected binding before acceptance and returns a JSON-compatible
+  receipt. Enforce the timeout. Return `queued`, `acknowledged` (durably stored),
+  `failed`, or `unknown`, with an optional `receipt_id`. Queuing is not durable
+  storage or recall visibility. Do not claim idempotency unless client effect IDs
+  are durably deduplicated by the backend.
+* `lookup_record(*, effect_id, expected_destination, timeout)` is read-only.
+  A lookup must never retry a write or redirect to a newly selected destination.
+
+Consumers declare the `memory.write_records` capability in their manifest and
+obtain user consent through the existing plugin capability flow. They also need
+their own explicit feature enablement; selecting `memory.provider` alone never
+authorizes additional records. Scheduled records additionally require the
+separate `memory.write_scheduled_records` capability grant in the owning profile.
+These controls do not change the provider's automatic conversation policy.
+
+The JSON-only `PluginContext` surface works in process and across the plugin host:
+
+```python
+status = ctx.memory_record_status()  # Config-only; provider support remains unprobed.
+context = {"session_id": run_id, "cwd": project_path, "scheduled": False}
+description = ctx.describe_memory_writer(context, timeout=5)
+if description.get("status") == "ready":
+    destination = description["destination"]  # Persist with the durable intent.
+    context = description["context"]  # Persist the host-resolved context too.
+    receipt = ctx.write_memory_record(
+        minimized_record, context=context, effect_id=effect_id,
+        expected_destination=destination, timeout=5,
+    )
+```
+
+If `cwd` is empty, the host resolves it from the owning profile's `terminal.cwd`.
+The ready description returns this resolved context; freeze it rather than
+resolving the current workspace again on every delivery attempt.
+Persist the context and destination with the intent. `lookup_memory_record` takes
+the same context, effect ID, expected destination and timeout; use it only for
+explicit reconciliation. Headless consumers use the public companion
+`hermes_cli.plugin_memory_records.MemoryRecordClient(home, plugin_id)` with
+`status`, `describe`, `write`, and `lookup`, under the same consent and scope rules.
+The plugin context fixes the owner home and plugin ID; these cannot be supplied
+by a hosted caller.
+
+The host owns fresh provider initialization, secret scope and cleanup, without
+constructing an agent or calling conversation/native-memory hooks. It supplies
+`agent_context="workflow"` for manual records and `"cron"` for scheduled records.
+Profile identity comes from the owning Hermes scope and the normal workspace
+namespace stays `hermes`; neither can be overridden by a plugin. The context can
+also freeze the verified origin's `platform`, `user_id`, `user_id_alt`, and
+`parent_session_id`. Omitted origin values are empty, and platform defaults to
+`workflow`. Providers apply their existing bank-routing policy to this explicit
+run context. Persist these values with the destination; changing them changes
+the binding and cannot redirect an existing intent.
+Redaction runs before provider dispatch, in addition to consumer-side data
+minimization. An unimplemented provider reports `capability_unavailable`; it
+never falls back to another provider or to MEMORY.md/USER.md.
+
+Calls have a maximum 30-second deadline. A timed-out writer remains quarantined
+in its owner-profile/plugin slot until cleanup ends, so callers cannot multiply
+stalled writes. A deadline never proves a write was absent: persist `unknown`
+and reconcile where supported instead of resending. The host checks expiry
+before acceptance, so late initialization cannot start an overdue write. Provider
+cleanup and any backend processing can outlive the caller's deadline. This API
+does not claim exactly-once delivery or search visibility.
+
 :::tip
 Memory providers are one of two **provider plugin** types. The other is [Context Engine Plugins](./context-engine-plugin.md), which replace the built-in context compressor. Both follow the same pattern: single-select, config-driven, managed via `hermes plugins`.
 :::
